{
  "id": "Compound::DB01116 - Gene::1143",
  "verbalization": "Trimethaphan is likely to interact with Chrnb4 through a series of molecular and functional connections. The evidence suggests that Trimethaphan binds to Chrna3, a receptor involved in nicotinic acetylcholine signaling, and that Chrna3 is part of a family of receptors that includes both sodium- and calcium-permeable subtypes. These receptors are then associated with Chrnb4, indicating a potential functional link between Trimethaphan and Chrnb4. The shared participation in these receptor subtypes suggests that Trimethaphan may modulate the activity of Chrnb4 through the regulation of nicotinic acetylcholine receptors. The consistent binding to Chrna3 and the subsequent involvement in receptor subtypes provide a plausible mechanism for the interaction between Trimethaphan and Chrnb4, although the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
